/-
# Lang.Contextual.Closure — the guarded contextual closure `(P, Ω, σ, D, support, Φ, t)`

The object of ACTIVITIES-PROPOSAL doc 2 §9, over an ABSTRACT calculus (its data `CalculusData` here, its laws
`CalculusLaws` in the proof module), so the theory
does not wait on a particular CMTT core. Two instances exist: the toy of
`Lang.Proofs.Contextual.Experiments`, and the CMTT core's, `Lang/Proofs/Contextual/CoreInstance.lean`,
which lands as the second commit.

Limits, stated: fillings are typed in the EMPTY meta-context (`HasTy [] Ψ M A`: no hole inside a
hole's filling); supports are one level (tokens, never another hole's selection), so a support cycle
is not expressible here; an environment edit touches every candidate (dependency sets are
sufficient, not minimal).

* `P, Ω` — the retained `Template`: a term typed under an open interface of holes `u :: [Ψ ⊢ A]`,
  each with its guard, budget, hypothesis tokens and POLARITY (`chooser`, `acceptor`). Kept apart
  from every environment: resubstitution needs it.
* `σ` — `Closure.sigma`: the current partial meta-substitution, the selected filling per hole.
* `D` — the selected candidate per hole (`Slot.selected`): WHICH derivation stands behind `σ`. Two
  candidates with one filling and different supports are one `σ` and two `D`s (provenance `ab + c`).
* `support` — `Closure.support`: the tokens the selected candidates rely on (`effSupports`: the
  declared supports plus the hypothesis tokens of every context variable the filling USES).
* `Φ` — `Closure.Φ`: residual obligations (derived from the statuses) plus `owed` (established by
  the accepted history and never derived away).
* `t` — `Closure.hist`: the accepted interaction history, append-only.

A candidate's status is computed by ONE function, `judge`, from the hole's checker and guard, the
world's environment and the liveness of the candidate's dependencies; the status is never the
proposer's opinion. Assumption tokens are immutable and versioned (`Token`); a world says which are
live. Withdrawal is a liveness change: it can suspend, it never refutes (`conflict` is reachable
only from the checker and the guard, which read no token).
-/

namespace Nanodregg.Lang.Contextual

/-! ## The abstract calculus -/

/-- A hole declaration `u :: [Ψ ⊢ A]`: the hole's name, its local context, its type. -/
structure HoleDecl (Ctx Ty : Type) where
  name : Nat
  ctx : Ctx
  ty : Ty

/-- The DATA of a contextual calculus the closure theory uses (its laws, with the typing relation,
are `Lang.Proofs.Contextual.Closure.CalculusLaws`). -/
structure CalculusData where
  Ty : Type
  Ctx : Type
  Tm : Type
  tmEq : DecidableEq Tm
  /-- Simultaneous meta-substitution `⟦σ⟧P` for a PARTIAL `σ` (unfilled holes stay). -/
  msubst : (Nat → Option Tm) → Tm → Tm
  /-- The checker: a proposed filling acquires authority only through it. -/
  check : Ctx → Tm → Ty → Bool
  /-- `uses M x`: the filling mentions hypothesis `x` of its context (free-variable occurrence). -/
  uses : Tm → Nat → Bool
  /-- The context without hypothesis `x`. -/
  drop : Ctx → Nat → Ctx
  /-- Strengthening `t ≡ t₀[π]`: `strengthen x t` is `t₀`, `weaken x` is `π`. -/
  strengthen : Nat → Tm → Tm
  weaken : Nat → Tm → Tm
  /-- The variable term for hypothesis `x`. -/
  var : Nat → Tm
  /-- The type a context gives hypothesis `x`, if it has one. -/
  lookup : Ctx → Nat → Option Ty

instance (D : CalculusData) : DecidableEq D.Tm := D.tmEq

/-! ## Assumption tokens and worlds -/

/-- An immutable, versioned assumption. Re-using an id at a new version is a NEW token: the
proposition a token stands for never changes. -/
structure Token where
  id : Nat
  ver : Nat
deriving DecidableEq, Repr

/-- A world: which tokens are live, and the guard environment (dependent-context parameters). -/
structure World (E : Type) where
  live : Token → Bool
  env : E

variable {E : Type}

def World.set (w : World E) (t : Token) (b : Bool) : World E :=
  { w with live := fun t' => if t' = t then b else w.live t' }

/-- A primitive change of world. -/
inductive Edit (E : Type) where
  | withdraw (t : Token)
  | assert (t : Token)
  | setEnv (e : E)

def Edit.apply : Edit E → World E → World E
  | .withdraw t, w => w.set t false
  | .assert t, w => w.set t true
  | .setEnv e, w => { w with env := e }

/-- Re-issue an id at a new version: the old token is withdrawn and a fresh one asserted. -/
def reissue (id old new : Nat) : List (Edit E) := [.withdraw ⟨id, old⟩, .assert ⟨id, new⟩]

def applyAll : List (Edit E) → World E → World E
  | [], w => w
  | e :: es, w => applyAll es (e.apply w)

/-! ## Holes, candidates, statuses -/

abbrev Party := Nat

/-- A hole's interface: its declaration, the hypotheses of its context backed by tokens, its guard
(the hole's own admissibility condition: the CHECKER of a candidate belongs to the hole, never to the
candidate), its cost budget, and its polarity. -/
structure Iface (D : CalculusData) (E : Type) where
  name : Nat
  ctx : D.Ctx
  ty : D.Ty
  hyps : List (Nat × Token)
  /-- Every token-backed hypothesis is a real hypothesis of the hole's context. -/
  hyps_real : ∀ h ∈ hyps, (D.lookup ctx h.1).isSome = true
  guard : E → D.Tm → Bool
  budget : Nat
  chooser : Party
  acceptor : Party

def Iface.decl {D : CalculusData} (I : Iface D E) : HoleDecl D.Ctx D.Ty := ⟨I.name, I.ctx, I.ty⟩

/-- Checked contrary evidence about ONE candidate (never about the hole). -/
inductive Evidence where
  | illTyped
  | guardFails
deriving DecidableEq, Repr

inductive Status where
  | verified
  /-- the listed supports are not live: what would have to be re-established -/
  | suspended (missing : List Token)
  /-- the listed blockers are live: the retained exclusion argument -/
  | pruned (by_ : List Token)
  | conflict (ev : Evidence)
  /-- correct, but its declared cost exceeds the hole's budget -/
  | uncovered (cost budget : Nat)
deriving DecidableEq, Repr

inductive Obligation where
  /-- hole `u` needs a (new) verified filling -/
  | refill (u : Nat)
  /-- candidate `i` of hole `u` waits on token `t` -/
  | support (u i : Nat) (t : Token)
  /-- the executed selection `i` of hole `u` must be settled -/
  | settle (u i : Nat)
  /-- the executed selection `i` of hole `u` lost its justification after execution -/
  | reconcile (u i : Nat)
deriving DecidableEq, Repr

/-- A retained candidate filling. `status` is the RECORDED status (the last judgment). -/
structure Candidate (D : CalculusData) where
  filling : D.Tm
  supports : List Token
  blockers : List Token
  cost : Nat
  by_ : Party
  status : Status

structure Slot (D : CalculusData) where
  cands : List (Candidate D)
  selected : Option Nat
  accepted : Bool

def Slot.empty {D : CalculusData} : Slot D := ⟨[], none, false⟩

inductive Event where
  | proposed (u i : Nat)
  | selected (u i : Nat)
  | deselected (u : Nat)
  | accepted (u i : Nat)
  | pruned (u i : Nat)
  | rederived (u i : Nat)
deriving DecidableEq, Repr

/-- The typed repair outcomes for one hole. The proposal's four are `replacement`, `strengthening`,
`alternative`, `suspended`; `kept` (the selected candidate stands, nothing to repair) is the fifth
case a total repair function needs. -/
inductive Outcome (D : CalculusData) where
  | kept (u : Nat)
  /-- `σ' = σ[u ↦ M]`: a verified candidate `j` with a DIFFERENT filling, offered to the chooser -/
  | replacement (u j : Nat) (M : D.Tm)
  /-- hypothesis `x` left the hole's context; the selected filling factors as `t₀[π]` -/
  | strengthening (u x : Nat) (t₀ : D.Tm)
  /-- the same filling through another derivation `j`: `σ` unchanged, `D` changed (applied) -/
  | alternative (u j : Nat)
  | suspended (u : Nat) (Φ : List Obligation)

inductive Refusal where
  | unknownHole
  | notChooser
  | notAcceptor
  | noCandidate
  | notVerified (s : Status)
  | accepted
  | notSelected
deriving DecidableEq, Repr

/-! ## The template and the closure -/

structure Template (D : CalculusData) (E : Type) where
  Γ : D.Ctx
  T : D.Ty
  P : D.Tm
  Ω : List (Iface D E)
  names : (Ω.map Iface.name).Nodup

structure Closure (D : CalculusData) (E : Type) where
  tpl : Template D E
  slot : Nat → Slot D
  owed : List Obligation
  hist : List Event

variable {D : CalculusData}

/-! ## Judgment -/

/-- The supports a candidate relies on: declared, plus the token of every hypothesis it uses. -/
def effSupports (I : Iface D E) (c : Candidate D) : List Token :=
  c.supports ++ (I.hyps.filter fun h => D.uses c.filling h.1).map Prod.snd

/-- Everything a candidate's status reads from the token world. -/
def deps (I : Iface D E) (c : Candidate D) : List Token := effSupports I c ++ c.blockers

/-- THE judgment of a candidate at a world. -/
def judge (I : Iface D E) (w : World E) (c : Candidate D) : Status :=
  if D.check I.ctx c.filling I.ty = false then .conflict .illTyped
  else if I.guard w.env c.filling = false then .conflict .guardFails
  else if (c.blockers.filter w.live).isEmpty = false then .pruned (c.blockers.filter w.live)
  else if ((effSupports I c).filter fun t => !w.live t).isEmpty = false then
    .suspended ((effSupports I c).filter fun t => !w.live t)
  else if c.cost ≤ I.budget then .verified else .uncovered c.cost I.budget

/-- The provenance polynomial of a hole at a world: one monomial (a product of tokens) per candidate
that passes everything but its supports; the hole is justified iff some monomial is all live. -/
def provenance (I : Iface D E) (env : E) (cs : List (Candidate D)) :
    List (List Token × List Token) :=
  (cs.filter fun c => D.check I.ctx c.filling I.ty && I.guard env c.filling &&
      decide (c.cost ≤ I.budget)).map fun c => (effSupports I c, c.blockers)

/-- Does the edit touch what the candidate's status reads? (Sufficient, not minimal: an
environment change touches every candidate.) -/
def touches : Edit E → Iface D E → Candidate D → Bool
  | .withdraw t, I, c => decide (t ∈ deps I c)
  | .assert t, I, c => decide (t ∈ deps I c)
  | .setEnv _, _, _ => true

/-- The context hypothesis an edit withdraws from a hole, if any. -/
def hypOf : Edit E → Iface D E → Option Nat
  | .withdraw t, I => (I.hyps.find? fun h => decide (h.2 = t)).map Prod.fst
  | _, _ => none

def holeTouched (e : Edit E) (I : Iface D E) (s : Slot D) : Bool :=
  (hypOf e I).isSome || s.cands.any (touches e I)

/-! ## The seven-tuple's derived components -/

def Closure.find (cl : Closure D E) (u : Nat) : Option (Iface D E) :=
  cl.tpl.Ω.find? fun I => I.name == u

def Closure.selectedCand (cl : Closure D E) (u : Nat) : Option (Candidate D) :=
/- Guarded contextual closure: Judge. Moved without statement changes from Closure. -/
import Lang.Proofs.Contextual.ClosureStatements

namespace Nanodregg.Lang.Contextual

variable {D : CalculusData} {E : Type}
  {HasTy : List (HoleDecl D.Ctx D.Ty) → D.Ctx → D.Tm → D.Ty → Prop}


/-! ## Tokens -/

theorem set_live (w : World E) (t t' : Token) (b : Bool) :
    (w.set t b).live t' = if t' = t then b else w.live t' := rfl

/-- **Immutable versioned tokens.** Re-issuing an id at a new version never re-enables the old
token: whatever was live before, the old version is dead afterwards. -/
theorem reissue_never_reenables (w : World E) (id old new : Nat) (h : old ≠ new) :
    (applyAll (reissue id old new) w).live ⟨id, old⟩ = false := by
  simp [reissue, applyAll, Edit.apply, World.set, h]

/-! ## The judgment -/

theorem judge_congr {I : Iface D E} {w w' : World E} {c : Candidate D} (he : w.env = w'.env)
    (hl : ∀ t ∈ deps I c, w.live t = w'.live t) : judge I w c = judge I w' c := by
  have hb : c.blockers.filter w.live = c.blockers.filter w'.live :=
    List.filter_congr fun t ht => hl t (by simp [deps, ht])
  have hs : (effSupports I c).filter (fun t => !w.live t) =
      (effSupports I c).filter (fun t => !w'.live t) :=
    List.filter_congr fun t ht => by rw [hl t (by simp [deps, ht])]
  simp only [judge, he, hb, hs]

/-- **The boundary lemma.** An edit that does not touch a candidate leaves its judgment unchanged:
the dependency sets are sufficient. -/
theorem judge_frame {I : Iface D E} {w : World E} {c : Candidate D} {e : Edit E}
    (h : touches e I c = false) : judge I (e.apply w) c = judge I w c := by
  cases e with
  | withdraw t =>
    simp only [touches, decide_eq_false_iff_not] at h
    exact judge_congr rfl fun t' ht' => by
      have : t' ≠ t := fun he => h (he ▸ ht')
      simp [Edit.apply, World.set, this]
  | assert t =>
    simp only [touches, decide_eq_false_iff_not] at h
    exact judge_congr rfl fun t' ht' => by
      have : t' ≠ t := fun he => h (he ▸ ht')
      simp [Edit.apply, World.set, this]
  | setEnv _ => simp [touches] at h

theorem blockers_empty_iff {w : World E} {bs : List Token} :
    (bs.filter w.live).isEmpty = true ↔ ∀ b ∈ bs, w.live b = false := by
  rw [List.isEmpty_iff, List.filter_eq_nil_iff]; simp

theorem missing_empty_iff {w : World E} {ts : List Token} :
    (ts.filter fun t => !w.live t).isEmpty = true ↔ ∀ t ∈ ts, w.live t = true := by
  rw [List.isEmpty_iff, List.filter_eq_nil_iff]; simp

theorem judge_verified_iff {I : Iface D E} {w : World E} {c : Candidate D} :
    judge I w c = .verified ↔
      D.check I.ctx c.filling I.ty = true ∧ I.guard w.env c.filling = true ∧
      (∀ b ∈ c.blockers, w.live b = false) ∧ (∀ t ∈ effSupports I c, w.live t = true) ∧
      c.cost ≤ I.budget := by
  rw [← blockers_empty_iff, ← missing_empty_iff]
  unfold judge
  cases h1 : D.check I.ctx c.filling I.ty <;>
  cases h2 : I.guard w.env c.filling <;>
  cases h3 : (c.blockers.filter w.live).isEmpty <;>
  cases h4 : ((effSupports I c).filter fun t => !w.live t).isEmpty <;>
  by_cases h5 : c.cost ≤ I.budget <;> simp [h5]

/-- `conflict` is decided by the checker and the guard alone: it reads no token. Its `illTyped`
arm is a refutation of typing (`conflict_illTyped_refutes`, by `check_complete`), its `guardFails`
arm a checked guard failure in the current environment. -/
theorem judge_conflict_iff {I : Iface D E} {w : World E} {c : Candidate D} {ev : Evidence} :
    judge I w c = .conflict ev ↔
      (D.check I.ctx c.filling I.ty = false ∧ ev = .illTyped) ∨
      (D.check I.ctx c.filling I.ty = true ∧ I.guard w.env c.filling = false ∧ ev = .guardFails) := by
  unfold judge
  cases h1 : D.check I.ctx c.filling I.ty <;>
  cases h2 : I.guard w.env c.filling <;>
  cases h3 : (c.blockers.filter w.live).isEmpty <;>
  cases h4 : ((effSupports I c).filter fun t => !w.live t).isEmpty <;>
  by_cases h5 : c.cost ≤ I.budget <;> cases ev <;> simp [h5]

/-- **`illTyped` is a refutation.** With a complete checker, a candidate judged `conflict illTyped`
does not type at its hole: checked contrary evidence, not a missing proof. -/
theorem conflict_illTyped_refutes (L : CalculusLaws D HasTy) {I : Iface D E} {w : World E}
    {c : Candidate D} (h : judge I w c = .conflict .illTyped) : ¬ HasTy [] I.ctx c.filling I.ty := by
  intro hty
  rcases judge_conflict_iff.1 h with ⟨hc, -⟩ | ⟨-, -, hev⟩
  · rw [L.check_complete hty] at hc; cases hc
  · cases hev

/-- `pruned` names exactly the live blockers, and there is at least one. -/
theorem judge_pruned {I : Iface D E} {w : World E} {c : Candidate D} {bs : List Token}
    (h : judge I w c = .pruned bs) :
    D.check I.ctx c.filling I.ty = true ∧ I.guard w.env c.filling = true ∧
      bs = c.blockers.filter w.live ∧ (c.blockers.filter w.live).isEmpty = false := by
  unfold judge at h
  revert h
  cases h1 : D.check I.ctx c.filling I.ty <;>
  cases h2 : I.guard w.env c.filling <;>
  cases h3 : (c.blockers.filter w.live).isEmpty <;>
  cases h4 : ((effSupports I c).filter fun t => !w.live t).isEmpty <;>
  by_cases h5 : c.cost ≤ I.budget <;> simp [h5, eq_comm]

/-- **The provenance bridge.** `provenance I env cs` is a polynomial over tokens computed from the
environment alone (it reads no liveness): one monomial per candidate that passes the checker, the
guard and the budget, its supports as positive literals and its blockers as negative literals. A
hole has a verified candidate at `w` iff some monomial holds in `w` (sum = alternatives, product =
joint requirements, negation = an exclusion reason). This is the bridge to `judge_verified_iff`: the
token part of the judgment IS the evaluation of a world-independent polynomial. -/
theorem provenance_iff {I : Iface D E} {w : World E} {cs : List (Candidate D)} :
    (∃ c ∈ cs, judge I w c = .verified) ↔
      ∃ m ∈ provenance I w.env cs, Monomial.holds w m := by
  constructor
  · rintro ⟨c, hc, hv⟩
    obtain ⟨h1, h2, h3, h4, h5⟩ := judge_verified_iff.1 hv
    refine ⟨(effSupports I c, c.blockers), ?_, h4, h3⟩
    simp only [provenance, List.mem_map, List.mem_filter]
    exact ⟨c, ⟨hc, by simp [h1, h2, h5]⟩, rfl⟩
  · rintro ⟨m, hm, hl, hb⟩
    simp only [provenance, List.mem_map, List.mem_filter, Bool.and_eq_true, decide_eq_true_eq] at hm
    obtain ⟨c, ⟨hc, ⟨⟨h1, h2⟩, h5⟩⟩, rfl⟩ := hm
    exact ⟨c, hc, judge_verified_iff.2 ⟨h1, h2, hb, hl, h5⟩⟩

/-- **The obstruction is real.** For every token-backed hypothesis `(x, t)` of a hole, the bare
variable `x` types at the type the context gives it and cites `t`: a filling that uses `x` depends on
`t`, so `uses ≡ false` cannot hide the obstruction. -/
theorem hyp_var_cites (L : CalculusLaws D HasTy) {I : Iface D E} {x : Nat} {t : Token} (h : (x, t) ∈ I.hyps)
    (c : Candidate D) (hc : c.filling = D.var x) :
    (∃ A, D.lookup I.ctx x = some A ∧ HasTy [] I.ctx c.filling A) ∧ t ∈ effSupports I c := by
  refine ⟨?_, ?_⟩
  · have := I.hyps_real _ h
    cases hA : D.lookup I.ctx x with
    | none => rw [hA] at this; cases this
    | some A => exact ⟨A, rfl, hc ▸ L.hasTy_var hA⟩
  · simp only [effSupports, List.mem_append, List.mem_map, List.mem_filter]
    exact Or.inr ⟨(x, t), ⟨h, by rw [hc]; exact L.uses_var⟩, rfl⟩

/-- **Weakening never produces the dropped variable.** No term typed in `drop Ψ x`, weakened back,
is the variable `x`: the general form, which bites for every calculus (de Bruijn included). -/
theorem weaken_never_var (L : CalculusLaws D HasTy) {Ψ : D.Ctx} {M : D.Tm} {A : D.Ty} {x : Nat}
    (h : HasTy [] (D.drop Ψ x) M A) : D.weaken x M ≠ D.var x := by
  intro he
  have := L.weaken_avoids h
  rw [he, L.uses_var] at this
  cases this

/-- **`drop` removes the hypothesis, for weakenings that fix the variable.** If weakening fixes
`var x` (named-variable calculi such as the toy, or the degenerate `weaken = id`), then `var x` does
not type in `drop Ψ x`, so `drop = id` with `weaken = id` is refuted. It does NOT cover a de Bruijn
weakening (CMTT's `shiftAt` sends `var x` to `var (x+1)`, so the premise is false there); the
general statement is `weaken_never_var`. -/
theorem drop_removes (L : CalculusLaws D HasTy) {Ψ : D.Ctx} {x : Nat} {A : D.Ty}
    (hw : D.weaken x (D.var x) = D.var x) : ¬ HasTy [] (D.drop Ψ x) (D.var x) A :=
  fun h => weaken_never_var L h hw

/-! ## Withdrawal never refutes -/

/-- **Losing a justification does not establish its negation.** A withdrawal neither creates nor
removes a `conflict`, with the same evidence. -/
theorem withdraw_never_refutes {I : Iface D E} {w : World E} {c : Candidate D} {t : Token}
    {ev : Evidence} :
    judge I ((Edit.withdraw t).apply w) c = .conflict ev ↔ judge I w c = .conflict ev := by
  rw [judge_conflict_iff, judge_conflict_iff]; rfl

/-- Any token edit (withdraw or assert) leaves `conflict` exactly as it was: only an environment
change (or a different filling) can produce or remove checked contrary evidence. -/
theorem token_edit_conflict_iff {I : Iface D E} {w : World E} {c : Candidate D} {e : Edit E}
    {t : Token} (he : e = .withdraw t ∨ e = .assert t) {ev : Evidence} :
    judge I (e.apply w) c = .conflict ev ↔ judge I w c = .conflict ev := by
  rcases he with rfl | rfl <;> (rw [judge_conflict_iff, judge_conflict_iff]; rfl)

/-- A withdrawal never creates a pruning: it only removes liveness. -/
theorem withdraw_never_prunes {I : Iface D E} {w : World E} {c : Candidate D} {t : Token}
    {bs : List Token} (h : judge I ((Edit.withdraw t).apply w) c = .pruned bs) :
    ∃ bs', judge I w c = .pruned bs' := by
  obtain ⟨h1, h2, _, h3⟩ := judge_pruned h
  have h3' : (c.blockers.filter w.live).isEmpty = false := by
    cases hx : (c.blockers.filter w.live).isEmpty
    · rfl
    · have hall := blockers_empty_iff.1 hx
      have : (c.blockers.filter ((Edit.withdraw t).apply w).live).isEmpty = true := by
        apply blockers_empty_iff.2
        intro b hb
        simp only [Edit.apply, World.set]
        split
        · rfl
        · exact hall b hb
      rw [this] at h3; cases h3
  refine ⟨c.blockers.filter w.live, ?_⟩
  have h2' : I.guard w.env c.filling = true := h2
  unfold judge
  simp [h1, h2', h3']

/-! ## Lookup -/

theorem find_name_self {Ω : List (Iface D E)} (hn : (Ω.map Iface.name).Nodup) {I : Iface D E}
    (hI : I ∈ Ω) : Ω.find? (fun J => J.name == I.name) = some I := by
  induction Ω with
  | nil => cases hI
  | cons J rest ih =>
    rw [List.map_cons, List.nodup_cons] at hn
    rw [List.find?_cons]
    rcases List.mem_cons.1 hI with rfl | h
    · simp
    · have hne : J.name ≠ I.name := fun he => hn.1 (he ▸ List.mem_map.2 ⟨I, h, rfl⟩)
      have hb : (J.name == I.name) = false := by simp [hne]
      simp [hb, ih hn.2 h]

theorem find_self {cl : Closure D E} {I : Iface D E} (hI : I ∈ cl.tpl.Ω) : cl.find I.name = some I :=
  find_name_self cl.tpl.names hI

theorem find_some {cl : Closure D E} {u : Nat} {I : Iface D E} (h : cl.find u = some I) :
    I ∈ cl.tpl.Ω ∧ I.name = u := by
  unfold Closure.find at h
  exact ⟨List.mem_of_find?_eq_some h, by simpa using List.find?_some h⟩


end Nanodregg.Lang.Contextual
## LANG-66 Contextual modal types, the guarded contextual closure and the hole rows: activities and continuations get their theory at the Lean level, and their first demonstration through the receivers (2026-10-22; ACTIVITIES-1, deputized by Ember; ROOT GO with three conditions; design `redregg/work/ACTIVITIES-DESIGN-20261022.md` §9–9.1)
**Ember's ruling.** "I want activities and continuations … promote those things into nano, because we would finally have
a firm theoretical, metaprogrammatic and proof-engineering basis to rest them upon"; then, on the first design and its
obend prototype: "I don't see the actual explicit things GPT wrote about, contextual modal type theory etc. … implement
this properly and thoroughly at the Lean level."
**Decision.** Three things land as one `lang:`; nothing on the wire moves, no package re-ids, no Store re-geneses.
1. `Lang/Contextual/Core.lean` + `Lang/Proofs/Contextual/Core.lean` (113 theorems): a well-scoped, simply typed Contextual
   Modal Type Theory (Nanevski–Pfenning–Pientka): types `base | arr | box Ψ A` (the contextual type `[Ψ ⊢ A]`), terms with
   de Bruijn variables, `box Ψ t` CLOSED to its ambient context, `letbox`, and metavariable occurrences `mvar u σ` carrying
   an explicit simultaneous substitution; one `HasTy Δ Γ t A` (with `HasSubst` and `Step`, the judgments, in the proof module `Lang/Proofs/Contextual/Typing.lean`) over a meta-context that is a FUNCTION from names to declarations (`MEnv`, so a partial fill leaves unfilled holes at their names); a total type checker `infer`/`check` proved SOUND AND COMPLETE; decidable term equality by hand; `msubstP` (named, simultaneous, partial filling) with `hasTy_msubstP`; the strengthening FUNCTION `unshiftAt` with `shiftAt_unshiftAt`, `hasTy_unshiftAt`, `uses_shiftAt`; ordinary and metavariable substitution with their lemmas
   (`subst_lemma`, `msubst_lemma`, `subst_comp`, weakening both ways); the proposal's factorization `strengthen` (a term
   not using a hypothesis is a smaller-context term under the projection); weak-head reduction with `subject_reduction`
   (its two redex cases ARE the two substitution lemmas) and `step_deterministic`. Teeth: `box_cannot_capture`,
   `mvar_blocked`/`msubst_unblocks` (an unresolved metavariable blocks inspection until a METAvariable substitution
   arrives, never an ordinary one: Hu–Pientka's layered discipline, operational in `letbox_needs_box`),
   `strengthen_needs_unused`, `no_step_under_box`, `box_ambient_free` (a box typed under one ambient context is typed under any other). The Bell's resumption is a toy: a continuation `box [base] …` typed under
   ANY ambient context, resumed by `letbox`, computed by `rfl`.
2. `Lang/Contextual/Closure.lean` + `Lang/Proofs/Contextual/{Closure,Experiments}.lean` (86 + 36 theorems): the GUARDED
   CONTEXTUAL CLOSURE — the proposal's `(P, Ω, σ, D, support, Φ, t)` — over an abstract calculus split as nano splits everything: `CalculusData` (a def module: Ty, Ctx, Tm, decidable term equality, msubst, check, uses, drop, strengthen, weaken, var, lookup) and `CalculusLaws D HasTy : Prop` (a proof module: msubst_lemma, check_sound, check_complete, strengthen_lemma, strengthen_factor, weaken_avoids, hasTy_var, uses_var; the last three anchor `uses` and `drop` so no degenerate instance satisfies them), `HasTy` a parameter, a template's typing the predicate `Template.Typed` (so the theory is calculus-independent, as Mini's `GuardedAdvice` was candidate-independent, and the gate's def/proof split holds: C3): immutable versioned assumption tokens, a
   hole's interface with its guard, chooser and acceptor (POLARITY), retained candidates with the reason each was set
   aside (`Status := verified | suspended | pruned | conflict | uncovered`), five repair outcomes, `judge` the one status
   function, `Derivable` SEPARATE from `Available`, `repair` and `fromScratch`. Theorems: conditional soundness
   (`conditional_soundness(_closure)`), repair correctness (`repair_agrees_from_scratch` under `Coherent`, kept reachable
   by `repair_coherent`/`fromScratch_coherent`), locality (`repair` RETURNS the candidates it re-judged; `repair_rejudged_eq_touched`: exactly the touched ones, a statement recompute-all FAILS; `locality`, `repair_carries_untouched`, `judge_frame`; `rejudged_only_touched` computed on experiment 1),
   `withdraw_never_refutes` (a withdrawal never acquires a negation), `select_requires_available`,
   `executed_prefix_blocks_replan` (no select, deselect OR prune of an accepted selection), `history_is_prefix`, `conflict_illTyped_refutes` (with `check_complete` an ill-typed verdict is a true refutation), `session_preserves_coherent`/`s1_coherent` (Coherent shown on an experiment state, from the session theorems), `provenance_iff` (the ATMS/semiring reading, THE BRIDGE between two independent definitions: `provenance` is a WORLD-FREE polynomial over tokens, supports positive literals and blockers negative, and a hole has a verified candidate iff some monomial holds at the world; `provenance_world_free`), `stability_class`/`valueGuard_iff` (which substitutions a guard's
   verdict survives), and the EIGHT EXPERIMENTS as named theorems over a concrete session: withdraw one of two supports,
   withdraw the last, withdraw a pruning reason, a cycle losing its grounding (NOT expressible in the one-level closure: a separate rule model, `unfounded_not_derivable`, over a world where an unrelated fact stays live; least fixpoint loses it, naive counting keeps it: both proved), a dependent-context change, replace a filling preserving its contract, correctness kept but a
   cost bound lost, re-apply an earlier filling.
3. `Lang/Journey.lean`: rows LH0–LH11, the hole as a surface package (`holeText`, the text a resident writes) through the
   real receivers: a candidate's supports are assumption-token ROWS on the hole object; a support re-pinned or withdrawn
   by its poster refuses a `select` BY NAME (`select: refused support0 moved` / `support0 withdrawn`), also over the view
   before the withdrawal; the candidate with the surviving support is selected (139 required steps / 179 ticks); a select
   after `accept` is refused `accepted`; a post-acceptance withdrawal leaves the selection; the over-cap candidate is
   retained `suspended "cap"`; the chooser prunes naming why. Plant (by hand, logged): the pin equality removed from
   `select` turns LH7 admitted and LH7b `rejected stale` — the kernel's row pin is reached exactly when the method's check
   is absent: derivability and admission are two judges, measured.
**What the Lean theory corrected in the design that was proposed** (and in `ACTIVITIES-DESIGN` §3–4, which §9 supersedes):
a method never sees its call's cell pins and reads rows under `self` only, so a support is a row under the hole object,
never a pin on another cell; `relaw` is refused `hasMethods` on a programmed object, so a pruning reason or guard
parameter is STATE changed by a method, not a law; `admit_comm` is insert-only, so selects do not commute by it; an
identical row upsert bumps the version (measured), so an exported `value` row must skip unchanged upserts; the guard
belongs to the hole, not the candidate; the outcomes need `kept` and differ in who may apply each (`replacement` is
OFFERED to the chooser, never applied by repair; after `accept` only `suspended (reconcile)`); repair-equals-from-scratch
needs `Coherent` and an explicit observable (the selection is history), and locality is a separate theorem.
**Price.** No landed theorem gains a premise, no landed statement changes: three new def modules, four new proof modules,
rows appended to one journey. `Lang/Proofs/Contextual/Closure.lean` is 1,236 lines in one proof module (law 8 asks for a
split; a follow-up `lang:`). SIZE: modules, lines and theorems rise, stated in the commit.
**Not in this landing, by name.** (a) The REAL-CMTT instance (`Lang/Contextual/CoreCalculus.lean`: `cmttData : CalculusData`, `holeEnv`; `Lang/Proofs/Contextual/CoreInstance.lean`: `theorem cmttLaws : CalculusLaws cmttData (HasTy ∘ holeEnv)`, every law discharged, `weaken_avoids` by `uses_shiftAt`, `check_complete` by the checker's completeness; `bell_template_toy`: the template `λx.u₀[x]` typed WITH the hole and not without, filled by `rfl`, typed with no holes left through the instance's `msubst_lemma`; `cmtt_strengthen_toy`: the real strengthening is not the identity) is BUILT and lands as the SECOND `lang:` right after this one (ROOT's condition 2); until it lands the closure theory is instantiated only abstractly and by its toy calculus. (b) First-class substitutions
(Cave–Pientka): cv task 01a1587d-9a98, with the two syntax options. (c) Open protocols with polarized holes and linear
resources (`Lang/Contextual/Protocol.lean`, the two-cell one-use-capability negotiation): a lane in flight, its own
`lang:`. (d) The `Std.activity`/`Std.hole` generators that make the Bell an instance: after (a)–(c).
**Alternatives.** (i) Build only the obend package and rows (the first design): refused by Ember as "half-assed"; the
theorems were the point. (ii) A kernel continuation cell kind: refused on price (the three totalities, every `readKind`
site, a `kernel!`) and on law 1 (rows are "keyed bounded things under an object"). (iii) Depend on Mathlib for the
category-theoretic reading (presheaves over contexts): refused by LANG-15/GATES-1; the stability class is stated directly
(`StableUnder`, `valueGuard_iff`). (iv) Import Mini's `IndexedProgram`/`GuardedAdvice`: law 11 (no twins); restated
smaller and, for the closure, over an abstract calculus rather than Mini's `Vocabulary`.
**Critique.** (1) The closure's supports are one level deep: a hole cannot support a hole; the cycle experiment is
therefore a separate rule-system model (`Rule`/`Derives`/`lfpIter`), not a closure over closures. (2) An `env` edit marks
every candidate touched (locality bites on token edits only). (3) The toy calculus's hole occurrences carry no explicit
`u[θ]`; the real instance is where the CMTT and the closure meet, and it is not landed here. (4) The theory is simply
typed; the proposal's dependent-context experiment is modelled by a guard parametrized by `n`, not by `Vec n`. (5) The
rows demonstrate one hole per object; many holes per object (the `waits` row design) and the `Std` generators are not
built. (6) 1,407 lines in one proof module (`Lang/Proofs/Contextual/Closure.lean`), a law-8 split owed as a follow-up `lang:`. (7) Named limits from the instance lane: fillings are typed in the empty meta-context (no hole inside a filling, so hierarchical refinement is not expressible); hole-name uniqueness lives in `Template`, not in `HasTy` (the instance takes the first declaration of a name); fillings compare syntactically, so β-equal fillings are different candidates.
**Meta-critique.** The first design mapped the proposal onto nano's existing vocabulary and called the obend package the
deliverable; an adversarial read found 8 HIGH defects in that mapping and Ember named the substitution for what it was.
The pull was familiarity: pins and rows were already proved, so I reached for them instead of the theory. The second pull,
in this landing, is to call the abstractly-instantiated closure "the CMTT closure" — it is not until `cmttCalculus`
lands, which is why (a) is named above and the commit message says so.

